REM =========================================================================
REM os8088 - apps/apple2/welcome.a2b   the Applesoft listing WELCOME.BAS is
REM tokenised from (docs/APPLE2-SPEC.md section 16.2).
REM
REM THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE AND `build/WELCOME.BAS` IS THE ARTEFACT.
REM tools/a2bas.py tokenises it against the token name table in the PINNED
REM Apple II+ ROM itself ($D0D0, AppleWin bin/A2_BASIC.SYM:805), so the
REM shipped bytes rebuild byte for byte and no token in them was typed from
REM memory.
REM
REM IT CLAIMS NOTHING THIS BUILD DOES NOT RENDER, which is CWORD's WELCOME.RTF
REM rule (LESSONS.md 1) one machine along: every screen it puts up is one the
REM package composes today - the 40x24 text page out of the machine's own
REM character generator, INVERSE, lo-res with MIXED's four text rows, hi-res
REM with the same, and the speaker. It does not use FLASH, which the CPU_8086
REM tier refuses and greys with its cost (APPLE2-SPEC section 7.8) - a
REM listing that said "this flashes" would be false on the machine this port
REM is aimed at - and it says where the COLOURS are rather than pretending the
REM window has any (section 13.1: colour is FSXM_VGA13 and needs a VGA).
REM
REM AND IT IS SIZED FOR A 4.77 MHz XT, WITH THE MEASUREMENT ATTACHED. At
REM 0.64% of a 1.02 MHz Apple (section 16.4.1) a loop of 1,600 PLOTs is many
REM minutes, so the lo-res picture is 32 HLINs and the hi-res one 7 HPLOT
REM lines, and every wait is a GET rather than a FOR/NEXT delay - a delay
REM loop tuned on a 386 is a machine that looks hung on an XT.
REM
REM WHAT THAT COSTS, MEASURED ON THE XT ITSELF and not estimated (section
REM 16.2.1, build/a2welcome.py, off the BIOS tick counter): the text screen
REM is 2-3 s from RUN, the lo-res screen 76.2 s and the hi-res screen
REM 78.2 s. Two runs agreed to a tenth of a second on both graphics screens;
REM the text figure is the noisy one, being short enough that the launch is
REM most of it. They were 6.0, 91.2 and 106.2 before section 4.3.1's slice
REM controller, which is where the difference went. Both graphics screens
REM draw PROGRESSIVELY - a band or a line at a time - so the machine is
REM visibly working rather than hung, and README.TXT says the same number to
REM the reader who has not opened this file. It is the emulated Apple that is
REM slow and not the window: this is what 0.64% of a 1.02 MHz machine feels
REM like, which is the one thing a demonstration on this port is FOR.
REM
REM Lines outside this header are the program. A `REM` line here whose first
REM word is REM and which carries no line number is a COMMENT and is not
REM tokenised at all; the program's own lines are numbered.
REM =========================================================================
10  REM  WELCOME - APPLE II PLUS FOR OS8088
20  TEXT : HOME
30  PRINT "APPLE II PLUS, IN A WINDOW."
40  PRINT
50  PRINT "40 COLUMNS AND 24 ROWS, DRAWN"
60  PRINT "OUT OF THE MACHINE'S OWN"
70  PRINT "CHARACTER GENERATOR ROM."
80  PRINT
90  INVERSE
100  PRINT "INVERSE TEXT"
110  NORMAL
120  PRINT
130  PRINT "PRESS A KEY FOR LO-RES.";
140  GET A$
150  GR : HOME
160  FOR C = 0 TO 15
170  COLOR= C
180  HLIN 0,39 AT C * 2
190  HLIN 0,39 AT C * 2 + 1
200  NEXT C
210  PRINT "GR: 40 X 40 BLOCKS, AND THE"
220  PRINT "FOUR TEXT ROWS OF MIXED MODE."
230  PRINT "COLOR NTSC IN THE MACHINE MENU"
240  PRINT "PUTS THESE IN COLOUR ON A VGA.";
250  GET A$
260  HGR : HOME
270  HCOLOR= 3
280  FOR X = 0 TO 279 STEP 40
290  HPLOT X,0 TO 279 - X,159
300  NEXT X
310  PRINT "HGR: 280 X 160, MIXED AGAIN."
320  PRINT "PRESS A KEY FOR THE SPEAKER.";
330  GET A$
340  TEXT : HOME
350  PRINT CHR$ (7);
360  PRINT "THAT WAS THE SPEAKER: THE"
370  PRINT "INTERVAL BETWEEN TOGGLES OF"
380  PRINT "$C030, PLAYED AS A TONE."
390  PRINT
400  PRINT "TYPE LIST TO READ THIS PROGRAM,"
410  PRINT "OR RUN TO SEE IT AGAIN."
420  END
